      "lineNbr": 455,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039371
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-31T01:29:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It was never getting cleaned even before I switched it to dist/tools...\n\nBut if it gets cleaned in each build it will always be missing files, if `compile-guest-tests.sh` cleans the `dist/tools` folder it would delete all the kernel files that were put there at `compile-kernel.sh`\n\nEven if every build step only cleans it\u0027s own files, then switching versions still leaves artifacts because the build files are also different.\nIt\u0027s also infeasible because if one step puts like 20 files into `tools/usr/lib` and another step puts 30 files into `tools/usr/lib`, then... What? Each tool needs to know manually delete every single file by name? And what if the names slightly change, or the amount of of files (new files/files removed)? Obviously the entire `tools/usr/lib`(just an example) can\u0027t be deleted because that will break the earlier tool\u0027s compilation.\n\nThe only valid solution I can see is for each tool to have it\u0027s own subdirectory under `tools/\u003ctoolname\u003e/root-fs-tree/` (i.e. you\u0027d have `tools/kernel/\u003croot-tree\u003e`, and separately `tools/nvme-cli/\u003croot-tree\u003e`, and etc\u0027) and that\u0027s just messy and seems like a solution to something that sounds like a non-issue (and something that has already been like that for years, just under other `dist/` folders). If there\u0027s an issue/artifacts, a dev can simply delete the entire `dist/` directory because it never gets cleaned anyways.\n(Can do if you insist).\n\n`run-ci` is the only place where cleaning the tools folder doesn\u0027t break anything because everything will be recompiled and copied again into tools while CI-ing",
      "parentUuid": "d7cbc35e_21593016",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 453,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 455,
        "endChar": 33
      },
